<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free State of Schaumburg-Lippe</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1946 CE · capital Bückeburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of Germany's smallest republics, a successor of the Bückeburg principality that resisted absorption until 1946.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-ii-prince-of-schaumburg-lippe">Adolf II</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-schaumburg-lippe">Schaumburg-Lippe</a> abdicated on 15 November 1918, and the principality became a free state, with a constitution adopted in 1922 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buckeburg">Bückeburg</a> as capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With around 50,000 inhabitants, the state was largely rural, with sandstone quarrying and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obernkirchen">Obernkirchen</a>. Proposals to merge with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> failed in the 1920s, and the state kept its small parliament until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazis</a> took power and placed it under a Reich governor shared with Lippe. Its tiny size made it a symbol of German particularism.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1945 it was in the British zone, and in November 1946 it was merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oldenburg">Oldenburg</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>. The former princely family still lives at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buckeburg-palace">Bückeburg Palace</a>, and the district of Schaumburg preserves the name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-state-of-schaumburg-lippe.jpg" alt="Free State of Schaumburg-Lippe" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_F%C3%BCrstentum_Schaumburg-Lippe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bückeburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Freistaat Schaumburg-Lippe</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=free-state-of-schaumburg-lippe" aria-label="Free State of Schaumburg-Lippe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4883 373 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free State of Schaumburg-Lippe lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5203 629L5203 629M5203 629L5300 622M5203 629L5300 622"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5203 629h0M5300 622h0M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5203 629h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5203 629h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bückeburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=free-state-of-schaumburg-lippe">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
